{
  "id": "Compound::DB00477 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpromazine, a typical antipsychotic drug, is shown to bind to Hrh4, which is involved in the activation of G-protein coupled acetylcholine or neurotransmitter receptors. This binding leads to the participation of Hrh4 in the activity of Chrm3, a receptor involved in the regulation of neurotransmitter signaling. The evidence suggests that Chlorpromazine may modulate Chrm3 through interactions with Hrh4, which in turn influences receptor activity. The overlapping pathways in both paths indicate a potential role for Hrh4 in mediating the effects of Chlorpromazine on Chrm3. The integration of these findings supports a mechanistic link between Chlorpromazine and Chrm3, particularly through the involvement of Hrh4 in receptor activation. The observed scores suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct interaction and functional significance.",
